window.GROWTH_INDEX = {"scenario":{"title":"Growth and sensor placement","question":"New houses connect somewhere. Where does the network run out of room, and where must a sensor be to notice?","what":["The whole catchment above chamber A, modelled in EPA SWMM: 158 pipes, 71 chambers, 643 connected properties counted from the published record rather than assumed. Load is steady. There is no storm and no event here: growth is a permanent change, so it is asked as a permanent change.","A chamber SURCHARGES when water rises above the crown of its outlet pipe. The pipe is full and the chamber has begun to fill. That is what a level sensor can see, and it is the trigger used throughout.","Growth by itself does not do this. At peak dry weather flow the worst reach in this catchment runs at 28% of full-bore capacity and the median at 0.8%, so filling a pipe on sewage alone would take roughly 2,274 dwellings where there are 643. Sewers are not sized for houses.","What fills them is wet weather. So each run carries an infiltration level as well, and the real finding is the one that follows: growth does not cause surcharge, it eats the headroom the network needs in order to absorb infiltration. The reaches that TIP, fine before the new houses and surcharged after, are what growth actually caused, and those are what a rollout has to see."],"read":["Pick a growth site. Red chambers are the ones that tip because of it; amber were already surcharged before any houses were added.","A site's capacity is how many dwellings it can take before anything new surcharges. A low number is a constrained connection point, not a bad chamber.","The sensor list is the fewest chambers that would see every growth scenario. Placement follows the bottleneck, which is rarely where the houses go."],"numbers":{"pipes":158,"chambers":71,"dwellings":643,"lpsPerDwelling":0.005787,"peakFactor":2.0,"iiRange":"0.25, 0.4, 0.55","stepRange":"+50, +150, +350, +700","baseSurcharged":0},"assumptions":[{"id":"L8","text":"**Sewage load is proportional to COUNTED dwellings**, at 500 L per dwelling per day (2.5 people x 200 L) and a peak factor of 2","status":"A","statusWord":"assumed","why":"Only the peak factor and the per-person figure are still assumed. The property COUNT is measured: layer 4 of the published service is one point per connected property and carries `PARCELID` on all 2,851 records. Supersedes L1 and L5 in `catchment.py`."},{"id":"L9","text":"Properties are attributed to the **nearest main**, within 40 m","status":"D","statusWord":"derived and checked","why":"Measured 16 Sep: 99.5% of the 2,851 points lie within 40 m of a main, median 7.0 m, and 14 are discarded as too far. A corner property can land on either of two mains, which is acceptable for load and is not hidden."},{"id":"L10","text":"**Infiltration and inflow is proportional to PIPE LENGTH**, default 0.25 L/s per 100 m, entering at each pipe's upstream end","status":"A","statusWord":"assumed","why":"Different basis from sewage on purpose: I&I enters through the pipe itself, through joints and defects, so length is the right measure, whereas sewage follows houses. \u26a0\ufe0f **Without it there is no question to ask**: at peak dry weather flow the worst of the 157 reaches above A runs at 28.3% of full-bore capacity and the median at 0.8%, so growth alone cannot surcharge this catchment at any plausible number of houses. The single most valuable figure a flow survey would replace."},{"id":"L11","text":"New dwellings add sewage but **no new I&I**","status":"A","statusWord":"assumed","why":"A new connection is new pipe in good condition. Over-optimistic for an old network where new development often means re-plumbing old streets."},{"id":"H8","text":"Chambers are SWMM storage nodes, 1050 mm diameter (0.866 m\u00b2), up to the lid","status":"A","statusWord":"assumed","why":"Standard chamber size, not recorded per asset. Controls how fast a shaft fills."},{"id":"H6","text":"Downstream boundary: the real pipe below D, draining to a **free outfall**","status":"A","statusWord":"assumed","why":"No backwater from the rest of the network, so levels at D are likely **under**-predicted."},{"id":"A3","text":"Inverts are flow-anchored: `START_INVE` is the upstream invert, `FLOWDIRECT` says which vertex that is","status":"D","statusWord":"derived and checked","why":"95% invert agreement at shared chambers this way, 35% the other. **Consequence:** normal flow direction is not an independent test; the new information is behaviour once pipes back up."},{"id":"A6","text":"Lid level: surveyed `ELEVATION` where published (10 of 360), else interpolated from the 1 m contours","status":"D","statusWord":"derived and checked","why":"Earlier check: about 0.23 m mean absolute error on the 10 surveyed lids. Chamber depth inherits it. Re-validate before quoting."},{"id":"H3","text":"Manning's n = 0.013 for every pipe, vitrified clay and PVC alike","status":"A","statusWord":"assumed","why":"`ROUGHNESS` is 0 on these records."}],"caveat":"The property count is measured. Everything that turns it into litres, and every litre of infiltration, is still assumed. Treat the ranking of sites and sensors as the result, not the dwelling numbers themselves.","qa":[{"short":"Why is the best sensor site not where the houses are?","q":"The two chambers that go first are small branches with almost nothing upstream of them. Why are they the answer rather than the trunk that carries the new sewage?","a":["Because they do not fill with the new sewage. They fill with backwater from the trunk below them.","MH4449118 has 12 pipes and 54 properties above it and its outlet is a 150 mm pipe. It discharges straight into a trunk chamber that is already surcharged. Growth anywhere in the catchment raises that trunk a little further, and the water has nowhere to go but back up the branch. At baseline the chamber sits at 39% of its crown; growth at a site it is not even downstream of drives it past 100%.","So the chamber behaves as a pressure gauge on the trunk rather than as a flow meter on its own catchment, and that is exactly what makes it a good place for a sensor: it responds to growth that happens somewhere else.","This is also the argument for modelling it in SWMM rather than screening it with Manning's equation. A steady normal-depth calculation has no backwater in it by construction, so it would show this chamber running a third full and quietly rank it as one of the safest in the network."],"evidence":"Traced at build time: the tipped chamber is not downstream of 59 of the 66 growth sites that tip it. Its first shared downstream node is the trunk chamber immediately below it."},{"short":"Why are the controls only a few fixed settings?","q":"Why can the wet weather level and the growth size only be set to a few values instead of being dragged freely?","a":["Because nothing is solved in the browser. Every position of both controls is a full EPA SWMM run that happened in advance, and the page is reading the answer rather than computing it. A continuous slider would mean interpolating between real answers and presenting the result as if it were one, which is worse than offering fewer honest positions.","The grid is small enough to be exhaustive: one steady state takes about two seconds, so every combination of wet weather level, growth size and connection point is a few minutes of computing. What you see is exact.","The settings are also coarse on purpose. The infiltration figure behind the wet weather control is itself assumed, not measured, so offering it to three decimal places would imply a precision the input does not have."],"evidence":"Every cell is a separate SWMM solve of the full catchment, held until the network settles."},{"short":"Does the growth size change where the sensors go?","q":"Does the recommended sensor placement depend on how many dwellings are added, or is it always the same?","a":["It does, and watching it move is most of the point of the control. But it moves in a way worth understanding, because the answer is not what you would guess.","A small development in dry conditions tips nothing at all, so there is nothing for a sensor to catch and the list is empty. That is a real answer, not a gap: it says the network still has room.","Everywhere else, ONE sensor is enough. Across all twelve combinations, every single one is covered by a single chamber, because in any given condition there is always one chamber that every tipping development passes through. Forty-two different chambers tip at some point across the whole grid, so the network is not simple; it is that each condition has its own common choke point.","What changes is WHICH chamber that is. Six different chambers take the job across the twelve combinations. So the placement question here is not how many sensors to buy, it is which condition you are designing for, and that choice moves the sensor rather than adding to it.","The earlier build had no control at all: every site was grown to its own threshold, which is one number per site, so the recommendation could never move. That was a defensible choice and it was also confusing, because it looked like an answer that ignored how much was being built."],"evidence":"Measured over all 852 site-scenarios in the grid. One sensor suffices in every cell where anything tips; the chamber chosen differs across cells."},{"short":"Why is there infiltration in a growth model?","q":"The question was about new houses. Why does the model carry a wet weather term at all?","a":["Because without one there is no question. Measured against full-bore Manning capacity, the worst of the 158 reaches here runs at 28.3% at peak dry weather flow and the median at 0.8%. Not one reach is over half full. Adding houses to that does nothing visible until the numbers stop being about this suburb.","Sewers are sized for wet weather, when infiltration and inflow enter through joints, cracks and wrongly connected downpipes. That is the condition that fills them, and it is the condition in which they spill.","So the honest claim is not that growth causes surcharge. It is that growth consumes the headroom the network needs to absorb wet weather. The two dials are separate on purpose, and they use different bases: sewage is proportional to dwellings, which are counted, and infiltration is proportional to pipe length, because it enters through the pipe itself."],"evidence":"Capacity check run over every reach at build time. Infiltration level is assumption L10 and is assumed, not measured."},{"short":"Where did the dwelling counts come from?","q":"Load used to come from an assumed density. What replaced it?","a":["Layer 4 of the published sewer service is one point per connected property and carries a parcel id on all 2,851 of its records. Each point is attributed to its nearest main, which puts 643 properties in this catchment.","The assumption it replaced put about 13 properties on every 100 m of sewer. Counted, it is 8.3 per 100 m here and 6.8 across the council, so the old figure overstated this catchment's population by about 1.6 times.","What is still assumed is everything downstream of the count: 2.5 people per property, 200 L per person per day, and a peak factor of 2. A calibrated flow per connection would retire all three at once."],"evidence":"Counted at build time from the cached layer. 99.5% of points lie within 40 m of a main, median 7.0 m."},{"short":"Does correcting nominal pipe diameter matter here?","q":"Diameter uses the internal figure where published and the nominal one otherwise. Should the nominal figure be corrected?","a":["No. Of the 546 mains publishing both, 82.6% differ by exactly zero, because vitrified clay is sized by its bore. Only plastic differs, and not by a constant ratio.","All 456 mains missing an internal diameter are also missing a material, so the field that would say which correction to apply is the one that is absent. Age stands in: none is dated later than 1968 and the earliest plastic main here is 1973. They are pre-plastic, so the right correction is zero, and applying one would introduce error rather than remove it."],"evidence":"Measured from the cached mains layer at build time. Per material: VC n=451 mean +0.00%; PVCU n=94 mean -4.22%; RC n=1 mean -11.11%"}]},"docs":{"assumptions":"# Assumptions register, `simulation/`\n\nA fresh register. Nothing here is inherited from `demo/` unless it is re-stated below with\nits reason. Every figure a run produces is conditional on these; quote results with the\nload split and scenario attached.\n\nStatus: **M** measured from data \u00b7 **D** derived from data by a stated method \u00b7\n**A** assumed, no data behind it.\n\n## A. What the data says is there\n\n| # | Assumption | Status | Why / consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | Network is the Walkerville council republication of the network operator's sewer asset register (mains layer 6, manholes layer 2), cached in `data/raw/` | M | Only public source with real manhole points and inverts on every main. `fetch_data.py --force` refreshes; results can change if the publisher updates. |\n| A2 | \u26a0\ufe0f **Plan positions are schematic.** `HORIZONTAL = \"Schematic\"` on 999 of 1,002 mains and all 360 manholes | M | Routes and lengths are drawn, not surveyed. Length feeds slope, friction, storage and travel time. Size of the error unknown. |\n| A3 | Inverts are flow-anchored: `START_INVE` is the upstream invert, `FLOWDIRECT` says which vertex that is | D | 95% invert agreement at shared chambers this way, 35% the other. **Consequence:** normal flow direction is not an independent test; the new information is behaviour once pipes back up. |\n| A4 | Pipe ends within 1 m are one node; a node is a chamber only if a published manhole point is within 1 m | D | Every manhole sits exactly on a pipe end; only 44% of pipe ends are manholes. |\n| A5 | Chamber floor = lowest pipe invert at the node, over the whole network | D | A chamber can sit lower than the modelled pipes, because of pipes outside the model. |\n| A6 | Lid level: surveyed `ELEVATION` where published (10 of 360), else interpolated from the 1 m contours | D | Earlier check: about 0.23 m mean absolute error on the 10 surveyed lids. Chamber depth inherits it. Re-validate before quoting. |\n| A7 | Diameter: `INTERNALDI` where published, else `NOMINALDIA` | M | 456 of 1,002 mains have no internal diameter. |\n| A8 | Pipe falls linearly between its two inverts | A | No intermediate levels are published. |\n\n## L. Loads: where the sewage comes from\n\n| # | Assumption | Status | Why / consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| L2 | All 157 pipes (7,781 m) upstream of A drain to A | D | Counted as a set of pipes. 9 chambers above A have two outlets, so part of that flow may actually leave elsewhere: A's load is an upper bound. |\n| L3 | `along`: each modelled pipe is cut into segments of about 15 m and its own load enters at each segment's top end | A | Stands in for house connections along the pipe. Barely matters here: the three study pipes are 189 m against 7,781 m upstream of A, so local load is about 2.4% of the total. |\n| L8 | **Sewage load is proportional to COUNTED dwellings**, at 500 L per dwelling per day (2.5 people x 200 L) and a peak factor of 2 | A | Only the peak factor and the per-person figure are still assumed. The property COUNT is measured: layer 4 of the published service is one point per connected property and carries `PARCELID` on all 2,851 records. Supersedes L1 and L5 in `catchment.py`. |\n| L9 | Properties are attributed to the **nearest main**, within 40 m | D | Measured 16 Sep: 99.5% of the 2,851 points lie within 40 m of a main, median 7.0 m, and 14 are discarded as too far. A corner property can land on either of two mains, which is acceptable for load and is not hidden. |\n| L10 | **Infiltration and inflow is proportional to PIPE LENGTH**, default 0.25 L/s per 100 m, entering at each pipe's upstream end | A | Different basis from sewage on purpose: I&I enters through the pipe itself, through joints and defects, so length is the right measure, whereas sewage follows houses. \u26a0\ufe0f **Without it there is no question to ask**: at peak dry weather flow the worst of the 157 reaches above A runs at 28.3% of full-bore capacity and the median at 0.8%, so growth alone cannot surcharge this catchment at any plausible number of houses. The single most valuable figure a flow survey would replace. |\n| L11 | New dwellings add sewage but **no new I&I** | A | A new connection is new pipe in good condition. Over-optimistic for an old network where new development often means re-plumbing old streets. |\n\n## H. Hydraulics\n\n| # | Assumption | Status | Why / consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| H1 | EPA SWMM 5 dynamic wave via `pyswmm` 2.1, 0.5 s routing step | M | The physics is SWMM's. Continuity error is recorded per run and the worst per experiment. |\n| H3 | Manning's n = 0.013 for every pipe, vitrified clay and PVC alike | A | `ROUGHNESS` is 0 on these records. |\n| H6 | Downstream boundary: the real pipe below D, draining to a **free outfall** | A | No backwater from the rest of the network, so levels at D are likely **under**-predicted. |\n| H7 | Water leaving a lid is lost: no ponding, no return | A | SWMM `ALLOW_PONDING NO`. |\n| H8 | Chambers are SWMM storage nodes, 1050 mm diameter (0.866 m\u00b2), up to the lid | A | Standard chamber size, not recorded per asset. Controls how fast a shaft fills. |\n| H9 | No entry, exit or junction losses; no benching or drop structures | A | Real junctions lose head, so backup is slightly **under**-predicted. |\n| H10 | In-pipe segment nodes (`along`) have a token 0.02 m\u00b2 area and can pressurise without spilling | A | A pipe has no opening between chambers. |\n| H11 | **Surcharge method is SLOT (Preissmann slot)**, not EXTRAN | D | Measured on the equal ramp at B. EXTRAN junctions stored **no** water while surcharged (shaft rose 1.3 m on 0 m\u00b3). EXTRAN storage nodes behaved as 3.54 m\u00b2 with 12.4% continuity error. SLOT gives 0.93 m\u00b2 against a true 0.87 m\u00b2, error 0.1%. `test_simulation.py` pins this. |\n\n## T. Stages (what \"reached\" means)\n\nWater depth above the chamber floor.\n\n| Stage | Level |\n|---|---|\n| Half pipe | half of (outlet pipe offset + outlet diameter) |\n| Surcharge | water above the crown of the chamber's outlet pipe |\n| Half shaft | half of lid minus floor |\n| Spill | SWMM reports flooding at the chamber |\n\n## E. Experiments\n\n| # | Assumption | Status | Why / consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| E1 | Ladder and growth runs hold load 120 min; the stage is judged over the last 10 min | A | Runs that have not settled are listed in the summary; their threshold may be slightly high. |\n| E3 | Each stage is reached monotonically as load rises | D | Checked on the coarse steps; any violation is printed as a warning in the summary. |\n| E6 | Float switch trips at the chamber's outlet pipe crown | A | Real float heights are set per site. |\n"}};
